# Learning Content Engine

A Python project for importing, processing, and serving educational
content and media as traceable, reviewed learning assets. Textbook PDFs are the
first content source, not the limit: the engine is intended to support
standalone images, SVG diagrams, and structured learning data as well.

The production direction is an ASP.NET Core modular monolith with a Python
ingestion worker, PostgreSQL, Gemini-assisted one-time textbook understanding,
and Groq for normal answer generation. This is a target, not the current
implementation; see the [architecture and implementation plan](./roadmap.md).
The rest of this README documents the existing Python prototype and its
optional content/annotation utilities unless a section explicitly says it is
planned.

The student-facing UI is a separate project. This engine accepts content,
questions, or submitted answers from its callers and returns structured,
grounded results. It is also separate from the Manim animation engine in
`AINEWPROJ`. Project documentation, source code, configuration, imported
assets, indexes, and generated data belong under
`AINEWPROJ/classdata/textbook_engine/`. The current SCERT PDFs remain in the
existing `AINEWPROJ/classdata/Telangana_SCERT_Textbooks/` corpus. The folder
name `textbook_engine` is retained for the project path; it does not restrict
the engine to textbook-only content.

## Content types and local data

The planned content model is generic: books and chapters are one way to
organize material, while a general source, document, page, media asset,
passage, annotation, activity, and citation can represent other educational
content too.

| Input | Initial/later handling |
|---|---|
| PDF textbooks and handouts | PyMuPDF extracts page text and renders pages during import; `extract-images` exports embedded image assets with page-placement references. |
| Raster images (PNG, JPEG, and similar) | Keep the source image, record its metadata, propose object boxes, and produce reviewed annotation data. |
| SVG diagrams and illustrations | Preserve the original vector asset and its text/shape data where useful. Rasterize a safe copy for detector input or preview when needed; choose and test the rasterization dependency during implementation. |
| Structured JSON and text | Validate and import content, labels, activities, and references using versioned schemas. |

Keep all project-managed imported assets, manifests, extracted text, crops,
annotation JSON, indexes, previews, and generated activity data under
`classdata/textbook_engine/data/`. Do not write annotations into or replace
source PDFs or images. The existing downloaded PDF collection is already
inside the broader `classdata/` workspace; refer to those files by path and
checksum rather than copying the entire corpus. Import other standalone assets
into the project data area when they are approved for use.

The current Python prototype does not use PostgreSQL. In the target
architecture, PostgreSQL indexes metadata and page text but does not replace
the original PDFs or versioned chapter packages.

Keep source attribution and permissions with every content source. Do not
publish or redistribute textbook pages, images, SVGs, or derived assets unless
their use permits it. Treat imported SVG as untrusted input: do not execute
scripts, load external references, or embed unsafe SVG markup in a client.

The available corpus currently includes English-medium PDFs for Classes 1–8.
Class 9 currently has only a Telugu-medium folder, so its English-medium books
cannot be processed until they are added to the corpus.

## Goal and first release

Given a selected source asset (starting with a PDF and selected page range),
the first useful release should:

1. Identify and validate a source asset and its metadata.
2. Extract or safely render its text and visual content, preserving a
   reference to the original file/page/region.
3. Retrieve the relevant text and page/image when a caller submits a question.
4. Use image understanding only when that question needs visual context;
   perform object localization only when the response must point to a region.
5. Save any generated locations as unverified candidates and require human
   review before they can be used as answer keys or student highlights.
6. Return grounded answers, citations, and only approved annotation references
   to a separate caller/UI.

The current release includes the offline processing pipeline and a local
Groq-backed student chat demo. It is a development UI for testing textbook
retrieval, grade-adjusted explanations, citations, and question-triggered page
image understanding; it is not the separate production student UI or a
multi-user service.

### Local Groq chat demo

The demo serves a local browser interface for Classes 1–8. It retrieves
textbook pages from the consolidated extraction index, adjusts its tutoring
instructions for the selected class, and includes page citations. If a
question asks about a picture, select its book and printed page first; the page
image is then sent to Groq for visual context. This demo does not draw or
verify object locations, and model descriptions must not be treated as
approved circles, labels, or answer keys. You can mention the number printed
on a page in your question. The engine maps that printed number to a PDF page
using a local OCR index; it never assumes that printed page 3 is PDF page 3.
If a page reference does not uniquely identify a book, choose the exact title
in the Textbook selector. The engine sends a selected page image to Groq when
the question needs visual context or its text could not be recovered. OCR
matches are confidence-filtered; uncertain and duplicate numbers are marked
for review instead of guessed.
With a textbook selected, asking "Explain chapter 1" supplies the detected
chapter's full extracted text to the tutor rather than relying on keyword
matching across a few pages.

### Prepared JSON chapters and images

The target versioned book package and page-record contract are documented in
[`HOW_TO_ADD_BOOK_DATA.md`](./HOW_TO_ADD_BOOK_DATA.md). The target format is
planned and is not yet loaded by the current prototype.

The current chat catalog also reads the page-oriented schema 2.0 from
`data/prepared_books/class_*/*/chapter_*.json`. It groups chapter files using
their `source_book` metadata and uses each page's printed/PDF provenance,
extracted text, page explanation, visual descriptions, and optional image.
This prototype format does not require `book.json`, an OCR pipeline run, or a
separate printed-page index. Restart the chat server after adding or changing
files. A referenced page image must exist under `data/` for the prototype to
send it to Groq; without it, text and supplied descriptions remain usable,
but visual claims are not confirmed.

The prototype also supports a separate manually curated `book.json` package.
For example, put it under `data/prepared_books/class_01/english/`:

```text
book.json
chapters/unit_01.json
images/class01_english_unit01_img001.png
```

`book.json` identifies the book and lists its chapter number, ID, and
chapter-JSON path. Each chapter JSON contains its original text, explanation,
questions, and image records. Use each image record's `image_id` as the stable
reference in section/question `image_ids`; use `file` for a path relative to
the book folder, for example `images/class01_english_unit01_img001.png`.
Question-to-image links let the tutor select the relevant original image
instead of attaching every image. The chat validates paths and references,
includes all supplied descriptions as text, and sends up to four selected
images to Groq. Groq receives image bytes through the server-side API request;
it cannot open a local file path or resolve an ID by itself.

The minimum `book.json` shape is:

```json
{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "book_id": "class02_science",
  "class": 2,
  "subject": "Science",
  "title": "Science (Prepared Content)",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "chapter_id": "class02_science_chapter01",
      "number": 1,
      "file": "chapters/chapter_01.json"
    }
  ]
}
```

Chapter JSON uses `schema_version`, `book_id`, `chapter_id`, `number`,
`title`, `summary`, `sections`, and `images`. A section can provide
`source_text`, `explanation`, `key_terms`, `key_facts`, `questions`, and
`image_ids`. Each image record needs a unique `image_id` and relative `file`;
`description`, `labels`, and `source_pdf_page` provide useful grounding and
citations. Every ID referenced by a section or question must exist in that
chapter's `images` list.

Prepared books are discovered when the chat server starts. Restart the server
after adding or changing package files. The current Class 1 English Unit 1
package is included as a working example. Its attached image records do not
yet include AI descriptions, so those original images are sent directly to
Groq when relevant; add reviewed descriptions and labels to improve retrieval
and answers.

Install Tesseract OCR for Windows and ensure `tesseract.exe` is on `PATH` (or
set `TESSERACT_CMD` to its full path). For example, install with WinGet:

```powershell
winget install --id tesseract-ocr.tesseract --exact
```

Restart PowerShell after installation. Process a newly uploaded PDF with one
command:

```powershell
content-engine process-pdf .\path\to\textbook.pdf
```

The pipeline preserves the original PDF and automatically imports and renders
all pages, extracts embedded images and text, applies local OCR to sparse or
broken-text pages, builds the printed-page index, and makes a completed upload
discoverable to the chat catalog. It can infer a class from the PDF filename
or first pages; use `--class-number 1` through `8` only when the PDF contains
no readable class information. Reprocessing the same PDF resumes or returns
its existing package instead of creating duplicates.

### Process one chapter on demand

Chapter extraction and explanation are a separate, explicitly invoked command.
This remains an optional PDF-based workflow; manually prepared JSON packages
are consumed directly and do not need this command. It does not run when the
application starts, and it does not perform OCR, printed-page detection, or
page-number mapping:

```powershell
content-engine process-chapter .\path\to\textbook.pdf --chapter 1
```

The command recognizes explicit `CHAPTER`, `UNIT`, or `LESSON` headings and
uses the next numbered heading of the same kind as the end boundary. If it
cannot identify both boundaries confidently, it reports an error rather than
guessing. It extracts only that chapter's text and local images, then sends
the extracted text to Gemini in manageable chunks for structured JSON
generation. The original extracted text is retained separately in the output,
and the generated chapter records its model and prompt-version provenance.
This first Gemini integration is text-only: images are saved and referenced,
but their visual contents and labels are not sent to or described by Gemini.
Set `GEMINI_API_KEY` in the project `.env`; the default model is
`gemini-3.1-flash-lite`, configurable with `GEMINI_MODEL` or `--model`.

To process another chapter, change the chapter number:

```powershell
content-engine process-chapter .\path\to\textbook.pdf --chapter 2
```

The default output is saved under
`data/processed/class_<number>/<book_subject>/chapter_<number>/` with
`chapter.json`, `extracted_text.txt`, `processing_report.json`, and an `images/`
directory. Repeat runs update the same named files instead of creating
numbered duplicates. The command sends textbook text to Gemini only when
explicitly run. Set `--output-dir` to choose another output root.

The pipeline selects Tesseract language data from the source filename (for
example, `Telugu` selects `tel`). Install the matching Tesseract traineddata
for each script the engine must OCR. If that language is not installed, the
engine preserves native PDF text and flags unreadable pages for visual review;
it does not substitute English OCR for another script.

Printed-page recognition uses OCR evidence at lower-corner badges. If at least
three confident OCR readings agree on a PDF-to-printed-page offset, unreadable
numbers can be resolved from that sequence. Other footer digits are ignored;
unmatched or conflicting pages remain marked for review in
`printed_page_index.json`. Review low-confidence results before relying on
them. This pipeline organizes and retrieves PDF evidence for the local chat;
it does not guarantee perfect OCR or verified object coordinates, image
labels, or circles.
Class 9 English-medium PDFs are not available in the current corpus.

The Groq and Gemini API keys are used only by the local server-side Python
process. From `classdata/textbook_engine/`, copy `.env.example` to `.env` and
set the keys for the features you use:

```text
GROQ_API_KEY=your_groq_api_key
GEMINI_API_KEY=your_google_gemini_api_key
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-lite
```

Never put either key in browser code or commit `.env`. The key is ignored by Git.
The demo sends the question, recent chat context, retrieved text excerpts, and
the selected textbook page image when its text is unreadable or the question
asks about a picture, to Groq. Do not
enter student names, school details, or other personal information. Review
Groq's current terms and your textbook usage permissions before using real
student data or distributing content.

Start the local server and open the printed address (normally
`http://127.0.0.1:8765`):

```powershell
python -m pip install -e .
if (-not (Test-Path .env)) { Copy-Item .env.example .env }
# Edit .env and set GROQ_API_KEY before starting the chat demo.
content-chat
```

The default model is `qwen/qwen3.8-27b`; override it using `GROQ_MODEL` in
`.env` if needed. `process-chapter` uses Gemini and requires `GEMINI_API_KEY`.
The service binds only to loopback. Use `Ctrl+C` to stop it.

## Product direction: a text-led, image-aware learning experience

The **student UI is a separate project** and is not part of this engine. This
project processes and indexes approved learning content, then provides a
backend interface for that UI: it accepts a question, selected content/context,
or activity response and returns structured answers, citations, feedback, and
references to approved image annotations. The UI owns screens, student input,
image rendering, tap handling, and display of the response. Text and images
must refer to the same source, page/asset, concepts, and approved annotation
records.

```text
Separate student UI
  ├── sends question, book/context, or activity answer
  v
Learning content engine backend
  ├── retrieves approved source passages and annotations
  ├── generates/checks a content-grounded response
  └── returns answer, citations, feedback, and approved figure/object IDs
  |
  └── response -> separate student UI renders text/image/highlights
```

### Student interaction modes

- **Ask and explain:** a student types a question. The service retrieves
  relevant passages from approved sources and returns a concise explanation
  with source/page references. A text-generation model may help phrase the
  answer, but it must be grounded in retrieved content; unsupported questions
  should be identified as not covered rather than answered from a guess.
- **Ask about a picture (planned):** retrieve the relevant textbook image when
  a question needs visual context, then ask a vision-capable model to explain
  that image alongside the related page text. The separate UI sends the source
  and page references; a model-generated location is not considered verified.
- **Point it out:** when an explanation refers to an object, the response may
  include a highlight reference only if it resolves to an approved annotation
  on the displayed figure. The separate UI draws a temporary circle, box, or
  outline over the original image. Generated text must never invent image
  coordinates.
- **Learn and practise:** show labels and explanations in learning mode; hide
  them in practice mode. The student can tap an object, reveal a hint, or
  request the correct region. Use an approved box as a first tap target; add
  masks only if actual object shapes are needed and the project later supports
  them.
- **Answer activities:** create fill-in-the-blank, multiple-choice,
  short-answer, matching, label-the-picture, object-finding, and carefully
  verified counting exercises. Each activity stores its answer rule,
  feedback/hints, language, and references to source passages or approved
  object IDs. An AI-drafted question is not automatically an approved
  assessment.

An explanation response should return text plus references the client can
resolve, rather than model-generated geometry:

```json
{
  "answer": "A tree gives the bird a place to rest.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "book_id": "book_001",
      "pdf_page": 12,
      "text_ref": "passage_012_03"
    }
  ],
  "highlights": [
    {
      "figure_id": "figure_012_01",
      "annotation_id": "object_004",
      "style": "outline"
    }
  ]
}
```

This is an illustrative future API shape, not an implemented contract. The
backend must verify that the cited passages and annotation IDs belong to the
selected content and are approved before returning them. The client obtains
the actual annotation geometry from the approved figure data and draws the
overlay on that figure.

### Content and runtime boundaries

- **Processing pipeline:** format-specific adapters ingest content. PyMuPDF
  handles PDFs; raster-image handling and safe SVG parsing/rendering provide
  additional asset inputs. LibreYOLO proposes candidate boxes for raster
  images; Pillow renders raster review previews; JSON stores provenance,
  annotations, and activity definitions.
- **Review/publishing:** only reviewed content is published to student
  experiences. Keep candidate, corrected, and approved records distinct.
- **Content API:** later serves approved text, source/page/figure metadata, assets,
  activity definitions, and citations. Keep all project-managed source files
  and derived assets under `textbook_engine/data/`, with stable references in
  metadata.
- **Learning runtime:** later evaluates deterministic activity rules,
  checks submitted answers, and returns feedback to the separate UI. Text
  generation can draft explanations, hints, or question wording from approved
  content; it does not decide visual locations or silently become the
  assessment answer key.
- **Student UI (separate project):** the production UI owns presentation,
  student interaction, and accessibility, and calls the engine backend. This
  project has a small local-only browser demo for development; it is not the
  production UI or its integration contract.
- **School integration:** use a separate adapter boundary for LMS/school
  systems. Evaluate standards such as LTI 1.3 for launch/identity and
  OneRoster or applicable LTI Advantage services for roster/grade exchange
  only when a target school requires them. Do not assume a particular SIS,
  LMS, or integration is selected. A later teacher experience can map learning
  content to the school's curriculum, assign activities to classes,
  and review class-level progress. Treat teacher, student, and administrator
  permissions separately.
- **Student privacy:** begin without collecting student identity. Before
  roster, account, or progress integrations, define minimum data collection,
  consent, retention/deletion, access controls, and school responsibilities.

## Chosen first-version stack

The current implementation uses these tools for the PDF-and-raster
vertical slice:

| Tool | First-version responsibility | Boundary |
|---|---|---|
| **PyMuPDF** (`pymupdf`) | Open PDFs, inspect pages, extract text and embedded images where suitable, and render pages to raster images. | Preserve source PDF page numbers and image/crop provenance. Scanned pages may need a later OCR stage. |
| **LibreYOLO** | Optional local candidate detector returning class labels, bounding boxes, and confidence scores. | Install the optional `detection` extra. Its weights/classes and performance on the actual content must be measured; scores do not verify detections. |
| **Pillow** (`PIL`) | Crop/save raster assets and render review previews with boxes, outlines where available, and labels. | Keep an unmodified source render; annotations are overlays or derived preview files. |
| **JSON** | Store manifests, image dimensions, source references, candidate annotations, review state, and activity references. | Use a versioned schema and validate paths and normalized coordinates. Keep image bytes out of JSON. |

PyMuPDF is the PDF adapter, not the universal content reader. PDF text is
extracted per page during import; run `extract-images` to export embedded
raster image streams once per unique byte sequence and record their page
placements. This does not extract vector drawings as separate images; the full
rendered page remains available for those. Standalone SVG
ingestion currently accepts a restricted, non-executable subset and records
metadata; it does not yet rasterize SVG for detection or previews. Structured
content receives JSON syntax validation. Keep format adapters behind a common
asset interface.

The current preview uses **bounding boxes**, not segmentation masks. Do not
describe a box as an exact object outline. If boxes prove insufficient for
touch selection, evaluate a separate segmentation stage in a later phase.
LibreYOLO is an optional detector wired into the local pipeline, but its
predictions remain unverified. PostgreSQL, OCR, and the production student UI
are not part of this prototype. Groq is used by the local chat demo for text
answers and selected-page vision context, but its descriptions do not verify
object locations.

## Processing pipeline

```text
Source asset (PDF / raster image / SVG / text or JSON)
  -> format-specific safe import and inventory
  -> normalized page text/media and provenance
  -> optional embedded-image extraction linked to source pages
  -> question-time retrieval and selected-page image analysis (local demo)
  -> optional detector suggestions and Pillow previews
  -> visual and human verification
  -> approved annotations and activity data
  -> response to a separate UI/client
```

| Step | Component | Responsibility |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Source adapters | Import PDFs (PyMuPDF), raster images, SVG, and structured text/JSON; retain source references. |
| 2 | PDF image extraction | Export embedded image bytes once per unique image and link every occurrence to its PDF page and position. |
| 3 | Question-time image understanding (local demo) | For a picture question, analyze the selected/retrieved page image; do not pre-label every image or treat the generated description as verified localization. |
| 4 | Candidate object detector (LibreYOLO) | Optionally propose supported object classes, locations, and confidence scores for raster images. |
| 5 | Annotation and preview (Pillow) | Save candidate coordinates and render raster previews with boxes and labels for visual review. |
| 6 | JSON generator and validator | Save versioned candidate annotations, source references, and review state; validate coordinates and asset paths. |
| 7 | Verification | Accept, correct, reject, or flag model proposals; prevent unreviewed labels from becoming answer keys. |
| 8 | Activity and response service | Build activities from approved content and return grounded answers, citations, feedback, and approved annotation references to callers. |

The optional LibreYOLO adapter produces **candidate** annotations, not verified
labels. Before processing a batch, check the actual weights, supported classes,
license, runtime, and results on representative images. If a requested label
such as “bird” or “house” is unsupported or unreliable, report it for review;
do not substitute a guessed box. Detection confidence is a review signal, not
proof that an annotation is correct.

## Current Python prototype layout

```text
classdata/
  Telangana_SCERT_Textbooks/       # Existing, read-only source PDFs
  textbook_engine/
    README.md                      # This overview and operating principles
    roadmap.md                     # Phases, dependencies, and acceptance checks
    pyproject.toml                 # Current Python package/dependencies
    src/textbook_engine/
      cli.py
      sources/
        base.py
        pdf.py
        raster_image.py
        svg.py
      detect.py
      annotate.py
      validate.py
      activities.py
      storage/
    tests/
    schemas/
    data/
      sources/                     # Approved project-managed source assets
      extracted/                   # Page renders, text, and crops
      annotations/                 # Candidate and approved JSON
      activities/                  # Validated activity JSON
      indexes/                     # Rebuildable local search/index data
      previews/                    # Derived review images
```

This describes the Python prototype, not the target production layout. The
current implementation is in `src/textbook_engine/core.py` and
`src/textbook_engine/cli.py`. Keep project-managed data beneath
`textbook_engine/data/`. The target production components and their
implementation sequence are in [roadmap.md](./roadmap.md); do not infer that
the current package layout already contains those planned services. Keep
LibreYOLO and its model weights isolated from PDF/JSON validation so basic
inspection and tests do not require downloading detector weights.

## Output package

Create one package per content source and processing version. For example:

```text
data/
  sources/
    Class_01_English_Medium_English/
      manifest.json
      pages/
        page_001.png
      figures/
        page_012_figure_001.png
    standalone_assets/
      classroom_diagram.svg
  annotations/
    figure_012_figure_001.json
    classroom_diagram.json
  activities/
    chapter_01.json
  review/
    annotations.jsonl
  indexes/
  previews/
```

Large raster images and model weights should not be embedded in JSON. JSON
should refer to actual files using package-relative paths. A validator must
check that every referenced file exists before declaring a package complete.
Generated data should not be committed by default; source documents, artwork,
extracted images, and secrets need deliberate Git and distribution policies.

## Annotation data contract

The schema should be versioned and validated before it is consumed. A minimal
candidate record could look like this:

```json
{
  "schema_version": "1.0",
  "source": {
    "source_id": "source_001",
    "type": "pdf",
    "path": "sources/Class_01_English_Medium_English.pdf",
    "sha256": "computed-from-the-source-file",
    "page": 12
  },
  "image": {
    "path": "figures/page_012_figure_001.png",
    "width": 1200,
    "height": 800
  },
  "annotations": [
    {
      "object_id": "object_001",
      "label": "house",
      "bounding_box": {
        "x": 0.12,
        "y": 0.25,
        "width": 0.3,
        "height": 0.35
      },
      "segmentation_mask": null,
      "confidence": 0.91,
      "status": "needs_review",
      "verified_by": null,
      "model": {
        "name": "LibreYOLO",
        "version": "model-version"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

The numbers above are illustrative only. Coordinates must come from model
output or human annotation on the actual image. Define `x` and `y` as the
normalized top-left position, with width and height normalized to the image
dimensions, origin at the top-left, and values in `[0, 1]`. Keep the source
page, crop dimensions, and crop-to-page transform so overlays can be mapped
back to the right image. Never generate a student answer key from
`needs_review` annotations.

## Storage and model boundaries

- **First milestone:** files plus versioned JSON are the source of truth. This
  keeps local experiments usable without a running database.
- **Later:** add PostgreSQL for book/page/figure/annotation metadata and review
  state. Keep large page images, SVGs, and crops under project data or object
  storage and reference them from the database.
- **Search, if needed:** evaluate PostgreSQL full-text search first; add vector
  search only when there is a demonstrated retrieval use case.
- **LibreYOLO:** local candidate detection only. Record the model/weights
  identity and output; send candidates through visual review before they can
  define an answer key.
- **Groq in the local demo:** generates age-adjusted explanations from
  retrieved excerpts and can describe a selected page image for picture
  questions. It is not a verified detector and must not invent object
  locations, citations, or answer keys.
- **Future database/API:** add only after local JSON processing and validation
  have a stable schema and a demonstrated need.
- Before distributing extracted textbook images or sending content to an
  external service, check reproduction rights, service terms, privacy needs,
  and school policy.

## How to use this project today

Create an environment and install the base project from this directory:

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install -e .
```

Install the optional detector only when you are ready to use LibreYOLO:

```powershell
python -m pip install -e ".[detection]"
```

Import a selected PDF page range, inspect the generated manifest, then edit
the page's annotation JSON to add **measured** normalized bounding boxes:

```powershell
python -m textbook_engine ingest ..\Telangana_SCERT_Textbooks\Class_01\English_Medium\Class_01_English_Medium_Mathematics.pdf --pages 1-2
$sourceId = "paste-source-id-from-the-ingest-manifest"
$manifest = ".\data\sources\$sourceId\manifest.json"
python -m textbook_engine extract-images $manifest
$annotations = ".\data\sources\$sourceId\annotations\page_0001.json"
python -m textbook_engine detect $annotations --weights LibreYOLO9t.pt --labels tree,bird,house
python -m textbook_engine validate $annotations
python -m textbook_engine preview $annotations --output .\data\previews\page_0001.png
```

PDF import already writes selectable text per page and a page render. Run
`extract-images` on the imported PDF manifest to export embedded image streams
under `extracted/images/`; the manifest records each image's page placements.
This extraction is independent of object recognition. The local chat demo
retrieves relevant text and sends a page image to Groq only when a picture
question is associated with a selected/retrieved page. This visual analysis
describes the page; it does not produce verified object coordinates.

To publish an annotation, set its status to `approved` and supply a non-empty
`verified_by` reviewer value. Coordinates must be measured on that image;
LibreYOLO's predictions remain `needs_review` and are not accepted as verified.

Other supported inputs are raster images, a restricted SVG subset, UTF-8 text,
and JSON. `content-engine --help` lists the commands. The PDF downloader at
`classdata/download_ts_textbooks.py` remains a separate tool and only downloads
source PDFs.

Current development status and exact limitations are tracked in
[PROGRESS.md](PROGRESS.md); future architecture and phases are in
[roadmap.md](roadmap.md).

## Success criteria

The pipeline is useful only when the result is both traceable and visually
correct. Before a pilot is considered ready:

- An asset can be traced to its source path and checksum; PDF-derived crops
  retain their source page mapping.
- Every annotation coordinate is within image bounds and renders over the
  intended object.
- Missing images, malformed JSON, invalid boxes, and broken asset paths fail
  validation with actionable errors.
- Unreviewed or rejected detections cannot silently become correct answers.
- Student hit testing uses the approved object region, not a model-generated
  label alone.
- Reviewers can identify and correct missed, duplicated, overlapping, or
  mislabeled objects.
- The pilot is measured on real pages in each target language before expanding
  to the full textbook collection.
